Ladybird resolves a relative URL given to tabs.create() or tabs.update()
against the extension, as Chromium’s ExtensionTabUtil::
PrepareURLForNavigation(), Gecko’s ext-tabs.js, and WebKit’s
WebExtensionContextAPITabsCocoa.mm do. It rejects a javascript: URL, a
file: URL, and an unparsable one with Chromium’s texts for them
(kJavaScriptUrlsNotAllowedInExtensionNavigations,
kFileUrlsNotAllowedInExtensionNavigations, and kInvalidUrlError), in the
same wording, since extensions compare those errors. It also rejects
another extension’s page, which Chromium’s navigation throttle blocks.

A call with no tab id acts on the active tab of the caller’s window, as
Chromium’s GetDefaultTab() does, failing with kNoSelectedTabError. And
an extension’s page may close its tab with window.close() whatever the
tab’s history holds, since extension_webkit_preferences.cc in Chromium
sets allow_scripts_to_close_windows for an extension’s own pages.
